- Closes https://github.com/godotengine/godot-proposals/issues/2954
- Requires https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/100145
- Follow-up of https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/100619
- (Mostly) Supersedes https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/74582

This PR adds `reserve()` to `CowData`, `Vector`, and `Array`. 
It achieves this by storing the capacity into the buffer, rather than inferring it from size.

This PR also refactors `CowData` almost completely. This was necessary for `reserve` to work properly, but has the side effect of neatly organizing functionality into smaller, specialized packages. This accelerates most functions.

## Explanation of behavior

`CowData` (and classes using it) currently have somewhat unusual behavior. This PR recreates current behavior, rather than changing it.
In particular:
- All element removal operations (e.g. `clear` and `remove_at` also shrink buffer. `LocalVector` for example only shrinks the buffer on explicit request (`reset`).
- Allocation sizes are deterministic by size (rather than depending on the number of objects being added or removed at once).
- Allocation size is grown by powers of 2, but measured as bytes rather than number of objects.

### New behavior

`CowData` is interesting in that there are usually 3 cases to account for (for size-changing functions). Each implies a different ideal strategy:
- There is no data yet.
    - -> Allocate new data, then add the elements.
- There is data, and we're the sole owner.
    - -> Do whatever is fastest on existing data.
- There is data, but there are other owners.
    - -> Allocate new data, copy elements directly into the spots where they're needed, and add new elements in the gaps.

If you review relevant functions, you should see most of them are structured in this way (with 3 separate branches). `resize` is an exception because it needs to handle both growth and shrinking efficiently.

## Explanation of code changes

I _really_, really wanted https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/100619 to be the large portion of work, and for this PR to be a small addition on top. 
Unfortunately, it was not possible, because current behavior of `CowData` necessitates differentiating harshly between _growth_ operations and _shrinking_ / _changing_ operations.
In particular, _growth_ operations (currently only `push_back`) are allowed to keep the previously reserved capacity, while _shrinking_ / _changing_ operations also shrink the backing buffer. It must be done this way to avoid regressions.

On the plus side, differentiating between growth and shrinking operations allows us to instantiate _far less_ code per call.
This shows in the final binary by the intense reduction of size.

I recommend reviewing this PR by looking at the result, rather than the changes. Almost all implementations needed to be changed to accomodate `reserve`, so trying to map them to previous behavior might be a bit of a waste of time.

Functions are documented by comments. If they're not enough, do not hesitate to ask! Names and comments are definitely open to discussion :)

## Benchmarks

I ran a pretty extensive benchmark to make sure there are no speed regressions. Some cases are marginally slower than before, but nothing is especially notable. In turn, a bunch of functions are noticeably faster than before.

The test code runs through several configurations of array size vs. iterations, and logs the time needed for each.

<details>

<summary>Test Code</summary>

```c++
	for (int64_t arr_size : { 4, 32, 512, 1024, 20000, 200000 }) {
		int64_t N = 200000000 / arr_size;
		int64_t N1 = 200000 / arr_size;
		{
			size_t time_spent_ns = 0;

			for (int64_t run = 0; run < N; run++) {
				auto t0 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				Vector<int64_t> vector;
				CRASH_COND(vector.resize(arr_size));
				int64_t *ptrw = vector.ptrw();
				for (int i = 0; i < arr_size; i ++) {
					// Test
					ptrw[i] = 5;
				}
				auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				time_spent_ns += std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(t1 - t0).count();

				vector.clear();
			}

			std::cout << "resize-ptrw: " << arr_size << std::endl;
			std::cout << time_spent_ns / 1000 / 1000 << "ms\n";
		}
		{
			size_t time_spent_ns = 0;

			for (int64_t run = 0; run < N; run++) {
				auto t0 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				Vector<int64_t> vector;
				CRASH_COND(vector.resize(arr_size));
				for (int i = 0; i < arr_size; i ++) {
					// Test
					vector.write[i] = 5;
				}
				auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				time_spent_ns += std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(t1 - t0).count();

				vector.clear();
			}

			std::cout << "resize-write: " << arr_size << std::endl;
			std::cout << time_spent_ns / 1000 / 1000 << "ms\n";
		}
		{
			size_t time_spent_ns = 0;

			for (int64_t run = 0; run < N; run++) {
				auto t0 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				Vector<int64_t> vector;
				for (int i = 0; i < arr_size; i ++) {
					// Test
					vector.insert(vector.size(), 5);
				}
				auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				time_spent_ns += std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(t1 - t0).count();

				vector.clear();
			}

			std::cout << "insert back: " << arr_size << std::endl;
			std::cout << time_spent_ns / 1000 / 1000 << "ms\n";
		}
		{
			size_t time_spent_ns = 0;

			for (int64_t run = 0; run < N; run++) {
				auto t0 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				Vector<int64_t> vector;
				for (int i = 0; i < arr_size; i ++) {
					// Test
					vector.push_back(5);
				}
				auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				time_spent_ns += std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(t1 - t0).count();

				vector.clear();
			}

			std::cout << "push_back: " << arr_size << std::endl;
			std::cout << time_spent_ns / 1000 / 1000 << "ms\n";
		}
		{
			size_t time_spent_ns = 0;

			for (int64_t run = 0; run < N; run++) {
				auto t0 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				Vector<int64_t> vector;
				CRASH_COND(vector.reserve(arr_size));
				for (int i = 0; i < arr_size; i ++) {
					// Test
					vector.push_back(5);
				}
				auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				time_spent_ns += std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(t1 - t0).count();

				vector.clear();
			}

			std::cout << "reserve push_back: " << arr_size << std::endl;
			std::cout << time_spent_ns / 1000 / 1000 << "ms\n";
		}
		{
			size_t time_spent_ns = 0;

			for (int64_t run = 0; run < N1; run++) {
				auto t0 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				Vector<int64_t> vector;
				for (int i = 0; i < arr_size; i ++) {
					// Test
					vector.insert(0, 5);
				}
				auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				time_spent_ns += std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(t1 - t0).count();

				vector.clear();
			}

			std::cout << "insert front: " << arr_size << std::endl;
			std::cout << time_spent_ns / 1000 / 1000 << "ms\n";
		}
		{
			size_t time_spent_ns = 0;

			for (int64_t run = 0; run < N; run++) {
				Vector<int64_t> vector;
				CRASH_COND(vector.resize(arr_size));
				auto t0 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				for (int i = 0; i < arr_size; i ++) {
					// Test
					vector.remove_at(vector.size() - 1);
				}
				auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				time_spent_ns += std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(t1 - t0).count();

				vector.clear();
			}

			std::cout << "remove_at back: " << arr_size << std::endl;
			std::cout << time_spent_ns / 1000 / 1000 << "ms\n";
		}
		{
			size_t time_spent_ns = 0;

			for (int64_t run = 0; run < N1; run++) {
				Vector<int64_t> vector;
				CRASH_COND(vector.resize(arr_size));
				auto t0 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				for (int i = 0; i < arr_size; i ++) {
					// Test
					vector.remove_at(0);
				}
				auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				time_spent_ns += std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(t1 - t0).count();

				vector.clear();
			}

			std::cout << "remove_at front: " << arr_size << std::endl;
			std::cout << time_spent_ns / 1000 / 1000 << "ms\n";
		}
	}
