To summarize:
- `resize` then `ptrw`-assign is ~3% slower than before.
- `resize` then `.write`-assign is 2% to 50% faster than before.
- `push_back` is 10% slower than before (small arrays) to 2x faster than before (medium to large arrays)
- `reserve` then `push_back` is 3% (large arrays) to 3x (medium size arrays) faster than regular `push_back`.
- `push_back` is 10% to 2x faster than `insert` at back (which was the previous implementation)
- `insert` at front can be 10% faster than before.
- `remove_at` at back is 1.5% slower (small arrays) to 30% faster (large arrays) than before.

I also tested `reserve` from GDScript to make sure it's still interesting there. It seems it can make a small but respectable difference of 20%:

```gdscript
func _ready() -> void:
	test(5, false)
	test(5, true)
	test(50, false)
	test(50, true)
	test(500, false)
	test(500, true)
	test(5000, false)
	test(5000, true)

func test(n: int, reserve: bool):
	var start := Time.get_ticks_usec()

	@warning_ignore("integer_division")
	for r in 10000000 / n:
		var a := []
		if reserve:
			a.reserve(n)
		for i in n:
			a.push_back(0)

	var end := Time.get_ticks_usec()
	@warning_ignore("integer_division")
	print("%dms" % ((end - start) / 1000))
```

